  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 222 AM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nMid-level ridging remains in control of the Southern and Central \nPlains early this morning. A convectively-enhanced shortwave is \npushing southeast across Missouri with showers and a few storms \ncontinuing just east of the forecast area. Outflow-cooled air has \npushed west across the entire area with observations showing \neasterly winds. Winds will increase from the south-southeast after \nsunrise this morning and work to mix out the cooler outflow air, \nleading to another hot afternoon with highs in the low 90s to near \n100 degrees. Short-term guidance suggests dewpoints mix out into the \nlow 60s or even upper 50s across central Kansas. Based on recent \nperformance, think this is too ambitious of mixing from models \nand have kept dewpoints in the low to mid 60s across central and\nnorth central Kansas and in the upper 60s to near 70 across far\neastern Kansas. This creates heat index values of 100-105 \ndegrees again this afternoon. Forcing is weak, but an uncapped \nairmass could allow an isolated shower or storm to develop this \nafternoon. Not expecting any severe weather, but inverted-V \nsoundings favor some gusty winds if a storm does develop.\n\nA pattern change occurs Tuesday into Wednesday as a potent shortwave \nejects east across the Northern Plains towards the Great Lakes, \nsquashing the mid-level ridge and shunting a cold front into the \nmiddle of the country. Ahead of the front, southwesterly winds will \nboost temperatures into the upper 90s to around 100 degrees again \nTuesday afternoon with heat index values of 100-108 degrees. The \nExtreme Heat Warning remains in effect through 7 PM Tuesday. Speed \nof the front is varied among guidance, but it appears the front \nshould be moving into northern Kansas by late Tuesday afternoon or \nevening with showers and storms developing along the boundary. The \nbest forcing is displaced north of the front which could lead to \nmore widely scattered storms as opposed to a solid QLCS. Shear is \nprogged to be marginal (20kts), but initial storms could pose a \ndamaging wind gust and hail risk. There is some potential for storms \nto evolve into line segments during the evening and overnight hours, \nbut overall confidence is low in severe storms at this time.\n\nThe front gradually moves south through the day Wednesday, with some \nshowers and storms continuing along the boundary. Cloud cover and \nnortherly winds will lead to a relatively cooler day behind the \nfront with highs in the 80s to low 90s. The pre-frontal environment \nwill still be hot and favorable for some marginally severe storms by \nWednesday afternoon, but the boundary may be completely through the \narea by the time convection develops. Will need to monitor the speed \nof the front to determine weather east-central Kansas will have a \nchance for some stronger storms and heat indices around 100 degrees. \nThe front will push through the area by Wednesday evening, bringing \nin the much anticipated cooler temperatures for Thursday! Highs in \nthe 80s on Thursday will warm slightly into the mid 80s and low 90s \nto end the week. Passing waves in the quasi-zonal flow lead to \ncontinued chances for precipitation into the weekend.",
